=begin pod

=head1 NAME

Testo - Raku Testing Done Right

=head1 SYNOPSIS

=begin code :lang<raku>
use Testo;
plan 10;

# `is` uses smart match semantics:
is 'foobar', *.contains('foo');    # test passes
is (1, 2, (3, 4)), [1, 2, [3, 4]]; # test passes
is (1, 2, (3, 4)), '1 2 3 4';      # test fails; unlike Test's `is`
is 'foobar', /foo/;   # no more Test's `like`;    just use a regex
is 'foobar', Str;     # no more Test's `isa-ok`;  just use a type object
is 'foobar', Stringy; # no more Test's `does-ok`; just use a type object

ok $number < 0;
nok $io-path.e, 'path does not exist';

# uses `eqv` semantics and works right with Seqs
is-eqv (1, 2).Seq, (1, 2); # test fails; unlike Test's `is-deeply`

# execute a program with some args and STDIN input and smartmatch its
# STDERR, STDOUT, and exit status:
is-run $*EXECUTABLE, :in<hi!>, :args['-e', 'say $*IN.uc'],
    :out(/'HI!'/), 'can say hi';

# run a bunch of tests as a group; like Test's `subtest`
group 'a bunch of test' => 3 => {
    is 1, 1;
    is 4, 4;
    is 'foobar', /foo/;
}
=end code

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Testo is the New and Improved version of C<Test> that you can use
*instead* of C<Test> to test all of your code and generate output in
L<TAP|https://testanything.org/tap-specification.html>, JSON, or
any other custom format!

=head1 EXPORTED ROUTINES

=head2 plan

Specifies the number of tests you plan to run.

=begin code :lang<raku>
plan 5;
=end code

=head2 is

Testo's workhorse you'll use for most testing. Performs the test using
L<smartmatch|https://docs.raku.org/routine/~~> semantics; i.e. it's
equivalent to doing C<($expected ~~ $got).Bool>, with the test passing
if the result is C<True>. An optional description of the test can be
specified

=begin code :lang<raku>
is 'foobar', *.contains('foo');    # test passes
is (1, 2, (3, 4)), [1, 2, [3, 4]]; # test passes
is (1, 2, (3, 4)), '1 2 3 4';      # test fails; unlike Test's `is`
is 'foobar', /foo/;      # no more Test's `like`;    just use a regex
is 'foobar', none /foo/; # no more Test's `unlike`;  just use a none  Junction
is 'foobar', Str;        # no more Test's `isa-ok`;  just use a type object
is 'foobar', Stringy;    # no more Test's `does-ok`; just use a type object

is 1, 2, 'some description'; # you can provide optional description too
=end code

Note that Testo does not provide several of
L<Test's tests|https://docs.raku.org/language/testing>, such as
C<isnt>, C<like>, C<unlike>, C<isa-ok> or C<does-ok>, as those are
replaced by C<is> with Regex/type objects/none Junctions as arguments.

=head2 is-eqv

Uses L<eqv|https://docs.raku.org/routine/eqv> semantics to perform the
test.  An optional description of the test can be specified.

=begin code :lang<raku>
is-eqv (1, 2).Seq, (1, 2); # fails; types do not match
is-eqv 1.0, 1; # fails; types do not match
is-eqv 1, 1;   # succeeds; types and values match
=end code

=head2 ok and nok

=begin code :lang<raku>
ok $number < 0;
nok $io-path.e, 'path does not exist';
=end code

=head2 is-run

=begin code :lang<raku>
is-run $*EXECUTABLE, :in<hi!>, :args['-e', 'say $*IN.uc'],
    :out(/'HI!'/), 'can say hi';

is-run $*EXECUTABLE, :args['-e', '$*ERR.print: 42'],
    :err<42>, 'can err 42';

is-run $*EXECUTABLE, :args['-e', 'die 42'],
    :err(*), :42status, 'can exit with exit code 42';
=end code

Runs an executable (via L<&run|https://docs.raku.org/routine/run>),
optionally supplying extra arguments given as C<:args[...]> or
feeding STDIN with a C<Str> or C<Blob> data given via C<:in>.

Runs three C<is> tests on STDOUT, STDERR, and exit code expected
values for which are provided via C<:out>, C<:err> and C<:status>
arguments respectively.  If omitted, C<:out> and C<:err> default
to empty string (C<''>) and C<:status> defaults to C<0>. Use the
L<Whatever star|https://docs.raku.org/type/Whatever> (C<*>) as
the value for any of the three arguments (see C<:err> in last
example above).

=head2 group

=begin code :lang<raku>
plan 1;

# run a bunch of tests as a group; like Test's `subtest`
group 'a bunch of tests' => 4 => {
    is 1, 1;
    is 4, 4;
    is 'foobar', /foo/;

    group 'nested bunch of tests; with manual `plan`' => {
        plan 2;
        is 1, 1;
        is 4, 4;
    }
}
=end code

Similar to C<Test>'s C<subtest>. Groups a number of tests into a...
group with its own plan. The entire group counts as 1 test towards
the planned/ran number of tests.

Takes a C<Pair> as the argument that is either
C<Str:D $desc => &group-code> or
C<Str:D $desc => UInt:D $plan where .so => &group-code> (see code
example above).  The latter form lets you specify the plan for the
group, while the former would require you to manually use C<plan>
inside of the C<&group-code>.

Groups can be nested for any (reasonable) number of levels.

